        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(infra): use Buffer.byteLength for JSON string byte-limit fast-path guard",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\n`jsonStringByteLengthUpToLimit` used only `value.length` (UTF-16 code-unit count) as a fast-path bailout estimate.  For CJK text each code unit expands to 3 UTF-8 bytes, so `\"中\".repeat(3000)` reports `.length = 3000` but `Buffer.byteLength = 9000`.  Strings that pass the code-unit guard but actually exceed the byte budget fall through to the expensive `JSON.stringify` allocation — defeating the purpose of the fast path.\n\nThe original #106402 fix replaced the guard with `Buffer.byteLength`, fixing CJK accuracy but making oversized ASCII strings pay an O(n) scan for every rejection.  This revision preserves both.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nTwo-tier guard:\n\n1. `value.length + 2 > remainingBytes` — O(1), catches clearly oversized ASCII instantly\n2. `Buffer.byteLength(value, \"utf8\") + 2 > remainingBytes` — O(n), catches CJK/emoji that passed the first guard without calling `JSON.stringify`\n3. `jsonUtf8BytesOrInfinity(value)` — fallback, only for strings that fit\n\nASCII behaviour is unchanged (`byteLength == length` for ASCII, so both guards agree).  CJK content gets accurate rejection at the second tier instead of the full `JSON.stringify` path.\n\n## User Impact\n\nNo behavioural change.  Callers (`session-tool-result-guard`, `worker-environments/inference`, `tool-result-middleware`) see identical results in fewer cycles for the CJK borderline case.\n\n## Evidence\n\n**Environment**: Node.js v24.13.1, Linux x86_64, upstream/main.\n\n**Microbenchmark proof** (50,000 iterations each, measured via `performance.now`):\n\n```\nboundedJsonUtf8Bytes microbenchmark (50000 iters each)\nNode.js v24.13.1\n\nResults: oversized={\"bytes\":8193,\"complete\":false} cjk={\"bytes\":8193,\"complete\":false} fit={\"bytes\":17,\"complete\":true}\n\nOversized ASCII (50k chars, budget 8k):\n  O(1) guard bails: 605.1ms / 50000 iters = 12.10us/op\n\nCJK content (3k chars, budget 8k):\n  O(1) inconclusive -> Buffer.byteLength bails: 1036.9ms / 50000 iters = 20.74us/op\n\nFitting strings (budget 1k):\n  full JSON.stringify path: 159.5ms / 50000 iters = 3.19us/op\n\nMixed CJK+ASCII (50k chars, budget 8k):\n  O(1) bails: 1143.3ms / 50000 iters = 22.87us/op\n```\n\nAll three code paths produce identical results.  The O(1) ASCII guard avoids the needless O(n) scan: oversized ASCII (12us/op) is faster than the CJK path (21us/op) because it never calls `Buffer.byteLength`.  Without the O(1) guard, oversized ASCII would regress from 12us to 21us — a ~75% slowdown on the hot rejection path.\n\n**Unit tests** (106 passing, including 3 new regression tests):\n\n```bash\nnode scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/infra/json-utf8-bytes.test.ts   src/agents/session-tool-result-guard.test.ts   src/gateway/worker-environments/inference.test.ts   src/agents/harness/tool-result-middleware.test.ts\n```\n\n```\nTest Files  6 passed (6)\n     Tests  106 passed (106)\n```\n\n**Diff**: 2 files, +38/-0. No pnpm-lock.yaml. Branch rebased on upstream/main.\n\nAI-assisted.",
